**Back and neck pain** are among the most common reasons people see a GP. Most people have back pain at some point, and it usually gets better on its own within **4 to 6 weeks**. When pain lasts more than 3 months it is called **chronic** or long-term pain.

## Symptoms

- pain, stiffness or aching anywhere from the neck to the lower back
- pain that gets worse when you move, bend, sit or stand for a long time
- muscle spasms
- **sciatica**: pain, tingling or numbness that spreads down the leg, caused by an irritated nerve
- pain in the neck that spreads to the shoulders, arms or head

## Common causes

Most back pain is **not caused by serious damage**. It often comes from strained muscles, ligaments or joints. Things that make it more likely include:

- lifting, twisting or bending awkwardly
- sitting for long periods, or poor posture at a desk or phone
- being inactive, or carrying extra weight
- stress, low mood and poor sleep, which can make pain worse and last longer
- age related changes to the discs and joints of the spine
- a **slipped (herniated) disc** pressing on a nerve

## Getting help

See a GP or physiotherapist if the pain is not getting better after a few weeks, is stopping you doing daily activities, or is very bad. They will ask about your pain and examine you. Most people do not need a scan.

Read about [treatment for back and neck pain](/conditions/musculoskeletal/back-and-neck-pain/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help straight away" %}
Call **999** or go to **A&E** if you have back pain with:

- numbness around your bottom or genitals
- problems peeing or pooing, or not being able to control them
- weakness or numbness in both legs

These can be signs of **cauda equina syndrome**, which needs urgent treatment. Call **NHS 111** if you have back pain with a high temperature, unexplained weight loss, or after a fall or accident.
{% /callout %}
